# Hermes G2 Privacy Policy

Hermes G2 is a user-controlled Android application. It does not operate its own
user-account, advertising, analytics, or billing service. Data flow depends on
the permissions, transcription provider, assistant backend, and optional tools
the user configures.

## Audio and transcription

Hermes G2 captures audio for user-invoked voice features such as the assistant,
Transcribe, notification replies, and voice typing. On-device transcription is
the default. Audio stays on the phone and is discarded after processing unless
the developer setting to save voice recordings is explicitly enabled.

Users may instead provide their own credentials for Deepgram, OpenAI Whisper,
ElevenLabs, or Soniox. Audio is then sent only to the selected provider and is
handled under that provider's terms and retention policy.

- Deepgram: https://deepgram.com/privacy
- OpenAI: https://openai.com/policies/row-privacy-policy/
- ElevenLabs: https://elevenlabs.io/privacy-policy
- Soniox: https://soniox.com/policies/privacy-policy

## Hermes Agent bridge

The Hermes Agent bridge is the preferred assistant backend. After transcription,
Hermes G2 sends the utterance text and limited turn context to the bridge host
configured by the user. Context can include foreground app/title, local time,
screen state, and device battery. The phone can expose eligible glasses tools to
that bridge over MCP; requested tool results may contain notifications, calendar
entries, media state, location-derived information, screen descriptions, or text
from an app whose tool has access to it.

Configured bridge endpoints must use encrypted `wss://` transport. The repository
does not provide or attest a public server deployment, certificate identity,
licensed adapter, model, retention policy, or host security. The bridge operator
and the user's Hermes Agent configuration control what happens after data reaches
that host.

External bridge calls are connection- and turn-bound. Sensitive R1 health tools
fail closed for the external bridge unless a separately trusted caller policy is
established; encrypted transport alone does not make a caller trusted.

Optional proactive actions are disabled unless the user enables the local master
gate. Eligible actions remain subject to proactive-safe flags and rate limits,
but they can still display information or perform consequences allowed by their
tool. Users are responsible for the host, tools, models, and instructions they
grant access to.

## Direct-provider fallback

When direct-provider mode is selected, Hermes G2 sends prompts, responses, tool
results, and relevant turn context directly from the phone to Anthropic or
OpenAI using the user's API key. If the downloaded on-phone model is selected,
assistant inference remains on the phone. Conversation and memory state may be
stored locally.

- OpenAI: https://openai.com/policies/row-privacy-policy/
- Anthropic: https://www.anthropic.com/legal/privacy

## R1 health data

The supported R1 health path reads bonded BLE data directly from the ring and
does not require an Even cloud account. Decoded battery, firmware version,
heart-rate, SpO2, HRV, activity, calorie, and timestamp data can be retained in
local application storage and exported only when the user invokes export.

Sleep decoding remains unavailable and does not surface guessed results. Raw
Bluetooth captures, official-app databases, health exports, detailed reverse-
engineering evidence, and firmware binaries are development evidence and must
remain outside the repository and ordinary app data path.

## Notifications, calendar, media, and files

Hermes G2 can read Android notifications, notification actions, calendar events,
media metadata, and user-accessible files after the user grants the relevant
Android access. This data is used for the glasses UI and explicitly invoked
assistant tools. Data returned by a tool follows the selected assistant backend's
data path described above.

Notification direct replies are delivered through Android's `RemoteInput` to the
notification's originating app. Hermes G2 does not operate an intermediary
messaging service.

## Location and external services

Navigate uses precise location and a user-provided Mapbox token for maps,
geocoding, and directions. While Navigate is active, location and the selected
destination are sent to Mapbox under its privacy policy:

- Mapbox: https://www.mapbox.com/legal/privacy

Weather may use coarse location and a third-party weather provider. Location can
also be returned by an explicitly invoked assistant tool when that tool is
available and authorised.

## Local secrets and storage

API keys, bridge tokens, Even/Mapbox/Nightscout/Roam credentials, and terminal
token-bearing connection records are encrypted with an app-scoped Android
Keystore AES-GCM key. Migration deletes a legacy plaintext value only after an
encrypted write and read-back succeeds. Explicit Clear actions remove both
encrypted and legacy copies. Uninstall or app-data clear destroys the Keystore
key, so credentials cannot be recovered; upgrade-in-place depends on preserving
the existing app identity and signing certificate.

Device addresses, conversation state, health history, and non-secret preferences
remain app-private but are not all Keystore-encrypted. Android backup is disabled.
Development preference pull/push helpers are not a supported credential backup
path and any output they produce must be treated as secret.

The bundled Local Counter compatibility sample stores only its numeric counter
in an app-private, package/hash-namespaced document. It has no network,
account, API key, WebView, raw filesystem, microphone, location, accelerometer,
notification, assistant-tool, or BLE capability. Its window menu can clear the
local document; app-data clear or uninstall removes it as well. Store downloads,
EHPK installation, remote code, and background sensor use are not implemented.

Never commit or publish:

- bridge, provider, Mapbox, Nightscout, or Even credentials
- `secrets.local.md`, `.env.local`, or pulled Android settings
- Bluetooth MAC addresses, serials, private IP addresses, or ADB identifiers
- raw health exports, official-app databases, or Bluetooth captures
- proprietary firmware binaries or completed consent/approval records

The repository `.gitignore` covers the known local paths, but developers must
still inspect every staged change and generated artefact before publishing.